# Line-ending policy for CRLF-sensitive CI fixtures.
#
# This repo has no global eol policy, so a fresh Windows clone with
# core.autocrlf=true checks EVERY text file out as CRLF. Most repo content
# tolerates that. These three fixtures do not, because each is read by a parser
# that splits on a delimiter and then compares the trailing field verbatim --
# there is no [[:space:]] trim in the way, so the CR becomes part of the value:
#
#   routing-cases.txt          validate-routing.sh reads it with
#                              `IFS='|' read -r trig expected`. The CR rides on the
#                              expected-agent field, so no fixture row ever matches,
#                              and the step-0 hygiene guard reports the cause.
#   expected-agent-sources.txt test-agent-capabilities-v08.sh reads it with
#                              `IFS= read -r agent`. With IFS empty nothing is
#                              trimmed, so the CR makes the path it builds,
#                              $CORE/agents/<agent>.md, unresolvable.
#   required-lines.txt         the same test takes needle="${row#*:::}" and runs
#                              `grep -Fq -- "$needle"` against a repo source file.
#                              The CR lands on the needle, so no mid-line literal
#                              can match and every row reports a false miss.
#
# `text eol=lf` pins the working-tree copy to LF whatever core.autocrlf says, so a
# fresh clone runs the same checks a hand-normalized worktree does -- the fixtures
# stop being a Windows-only red build. The CRLF guard in validate-routing.sh stays
# load-bearing: it still fires on a genuinely contaminated fixture (an editor that
# rewrites line endings, a bad merge, an archive extracted on Windows).
#
# Deliberately NOT pinned -- the rest of scripts/ci/fixtures/. Each was run against
# a simulated fresh core.autocrlf=true checkout and behaved identically to the LF
# worktree, because every consumer normalizes or parses the CR away:
#   rule-presence/**     validate-rule-presence.sh collapses [[:space:]] (which
#                        includes CR) to a single space before it matches.
#   release-ledger/**    the ledger's awk pass trims [[:space:]] off every cell.
#   check-route/**       Python json.loads treats CR as whitespace; the malformed
#                        model-map trust boundary still exits 2 with no JSON line.
#   memory-journalist/** read as an opaque text blob, matched by substring.
# A NEW fixture consumed by an IFS-split reader or a byte-comparison guard belongs
# in the list below -- add it here rather than letting the next Windows-only red
# build teach someone to ignore a real failure.
#
# CORRECTION 2026-10-03 -- manifest-version/** was listed here as safe on the
# grounds that "Python json on the legacy manifest, no byte comparison on that
# path." That is wrong, and it cost a Windows-only red build. In
# test-manifest-version.sh fixture 5 the file legacy/file.txt is copied into BOTH
# roles: once as $R5/src/file.txt (the source tree package-manifest hashes) and
# once as $D5/.sefi-agents-manifest.json. package-manifest.sh compares installed
# bytes against the recorded hash, so the CR that core.autocrlf adds to the
# working-tree copy changes the hash and the diff reports
# "drift in installed files: file.txt" -- asserting the fixture is byte-identical
# to itself. Verified on Windows: the committed blob is LF, the checkout is CRLF.
.gitattributes text eol=lf
plugins/sefi-core/scripts/ci/fixtures/routing-cases.txt text eol=lf
plugins/sefi-core/scripts/ci/fixtures/agent-capabilities-v08/expected-agent-sources.txt text eol=lf
plugins/sefi-core/scripts/ci/fixtures/agent-capabilities-v08/required-lines.txt text eol=lf
plugins/sefi-core/scripts/ci/fixtures/manifest-version/legacy/file.txt text eol=lf

# benchmarks/sandbox/deploy.sh is byte-compared, not read as prose.
# check_sh-strict-mode.sh `cmp`s the trial's copy (LF: sandbox.py forces
# core.autocrlf=false and the arm writes explicit LF joins) against its OWN repo
# copy with the strict line stripped. Under core.autocrlf=true that baseline sits in
# the working tree as CRLF, so cmp disagrees on a byte the test never meant to compare
# and the case fails as "content changed beyond the strict-mode insertion" -- the one
# benchmark failure that reproduces on a pristine checkout. Same class as the fixture
# pins above: hold the working tree at LF so a Windows red build stops being a red build.
benchmarks/sandbox/deploy.sh text eol=lf

# The benchmark oracle fingerprints pin the CONTENT of each case's prompt and check
# script, and cases.json records a hash computed over their LF bytes. Under
# core.autocrlf=true the working tree served CRLF, so test-benchmark-oracles.sh hashed
# different bytes than the fingerprint was recorded from and reported "stale fingerprint"
# for the case -- a fixture conversion, not stale content. Measured: for all three cases
# the LF-normalized hash equals the recorded value exactly, while the working-tree hash
# matches none of them.
#
# Pin the whole benchmark tree rather than three named files: the hash contract is a
# property of every case, not of the three that failed first, and a per-file list would
# leave the next added case exposed to the same red.
benchmarks/** text eol=lf
